# ── Cognitive dissonance theory ──
card("cognitive-dissonance-theory", "Cognitive dissonance theory",
  V("W4300107936"),
  "Holding two inconsistent cognitions (beliefs, attitudes, knowledge of one's own behaviour) creates an unpleasant state, dissonance, that people are motivated to reduce. They do it by changing a belief, adding justifying thoughts, or changing behaviour. A classic finding: people paid little to argue for something come to believe it more than people paid a lot.",
  ["Why do people justify choices, keep beliefs despite contrary evidence, or change attitudes after acting?",
   "How do consumers, voters or professionals deal with inconsistency between values and actions?",
   "Typical data: experiments, surveys on attitudes before and after decisions, interviews on justification."],
  ["Using 'dissonance' for any discomfort or disagreement.",
   "Not showing an actual inconsistency between cognitions the person holds.",
   "Ignoring the later revisions (self-consistency, self-affirmation, aversive consequences)."],
  [("refinement", V("W2017680376"), "The forced-compliance experiment: insufficient justification leads to attitude change."),
   ("refinement", V("W1584033246"), "Dissonance arises from responsibility for aversive consequences, not inconsistency alone."),
   ("refinement", V("W2123716662"), "Shows dissonance is experienced as psychological discomfort.")],
  [("Self-affirmation", "Affirming other important values can reduce the need to resolve dissonance.", V("W1935421882"))],
  [("Fifty years of research and revisions, with continued support for the original theory.", V("W2161028505")),
   ("Evidence that dissonance is a real state of psychological discomfort, as the theory claims.", V("W2123716662"))],
  [("Many dissonance findings can be explained as self-perception: people infer their attitudes from their behaviour, without any aversive state.", V("W2138612226")),
   ("Inconsistency alone may not be enough: dissonance may require feeling responsible for aversive consequences.", V("W1584033246"))],
  [("original", V("W4300107936"), None, "The founding book.", "What two cognitions are in conflict for the people you study?"),
   ("refinement", V("W2017680376"), None, "The classic experiment; short.", "How does justification work in your setting?"),
   ("review", V("W2161028505"), None, "A review of the revisions and where the theory stands.", "Which version of the theory are you using?")])
